ror":false,"num_turns":80,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"Cycle 131 is done: the first half of the ring-buffer slot writes (P3b-1) is built and is now the work VI. It is checked structurally only — it is broken by design and has never been run.\n\n**What happened**\n- **Tunnel-naming rule measured (131-1, PASS):** last cycle's scratch run stopped because the simulator predicted the wrong name for a new tunnel. This time the rule was worked out from the 18 tunnel-creating crossings recorded on disk, instead of guessed. When the source is a subVI, the tunnel takes the name of the source terminal (`Image Out`). The simulator now uses this rule and its self-tests pass (105/0). Both halves of the plan were re-finalized, and P3b-1's offline dry run and pre-run checks pass.\n- **Gate false positives cleared (131-2, PASS):** the hook that wrongly held offline self-tests now classifies commands by what they actually run. Before switching it on, I checked it against past cases: all 5 false positives now pass, and all 53 recorded LabVIEW launches are still held. fp-22/24/25/26/27 are drained and the `guard_cycle` build-detection pattern is fixed.\n- **Scratch run on a copy (131-3):** all 22 gates passed and both tunnel names came out as predicted. Peak memory was 680.8 MB, over the 675 MB planning margin, because the final whole-VI read cost 17.4 MB more than the model assumes. LabVIEW's memory error comes at about 695 MB, so I set the launch's memory check at 690 MB (PD272).\n- **One real launch (131-4/5):** the scratch Error List had 53 items, not the predicted 54. One extra loose wire end was cleared on a net the plan rebuilds. A loose end executes nothing, so the computation is unchanged. P3b-1 was launched once: 22/0 gates, 680.4 MB, saved as `claudeDev\\D1_ring_p3b1_20261002_060910.vi` (md5 9d7bf287).\n- **Final Error List read (131-6, PASS):** 53 items. The only difference from P3a is two fewer loose wire ends (24 → 22). That met the acceptance condition, so `current-bed:` now points to P3b-1. That makes 3 of the 6 allowed broken intermediate files.\n\n**Retrospective:** it found no violation (`VIOLATION: none`). Avoidable cost was about 20 minutes. The main one: card 131-5 should already have counted removed Error List items by category, which would have saved the sixth dispatch. Two of its follow-ups are now in STATUS NEXT for cycle 132:\n- A diagnostic script printed PASS without checking anything; its PASS must depend on its check.\n- One removed wire (`w27378`) is recorded in the prediction file without a measurement. P3b-2's expected Error List will be computed from the simulator instead of typed.\n\nThe cycle used all six dispatches allowed per session.\n\n**Next cycle (132):** two cards together:\n- **Offline tooling card:** adds the measured final-read memory to the memory model and an explicit tunnel-name gate. It also drains the remaining stage-prerun false positives (fp-19/20/21) and closes fp-28 as a correct refusal.\n- **LabVIEW card:** reads the P3b-1 file's real graph.\n\nAfter both: P3b-2 is re-bound to that graph, checked offline, run once on a copy, then launched once. That would make 4 of 6 broken files.\n\nNo new questions for you. D-2026-10-02-01 (a check that stops a helper agent from finishing while its own LabVIEW run is still going) is still open.","ttft_ms":3788,"type":"result","duration_ms":7054877,"uuid":"d98344cd-55a5-42d5-b276-c911eb6920c7","ttft_stream_ms":3499,"time_to_request_ms":232,"first_content_frame_ms":3500,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
